.TH COMMAND-CENTER 1 "October 2026" "Command Center 2.0.0"
.SH NAME
command-center \- native Debian host firewall and server administration tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B command-center
.RI [ options ] " command " [ arguments ]
.br
.B cm
.RI [ options ] " command " [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Command Center manages two owned nftables inet tables, OpenSSH
authentication protection, server reports, journal views and Debian
unattended-update controls. The optional shortcut package supplies cm.
.PP
Installation does not start or enable CM. A fresh start preserves
existing filtering behavior. Explicit rules/defaults/profiles introduce
restrictions. Other applications' tables are never flushed or modified.
CM manages host input/output, not forwarding/NAT or bridge-container
published ports. An accept cannot override another table's drop.
.SH FIREWALL
.TP
.B allow|deny|reject|limit [in|out] SERVICE [OPTIONS]
Direction defaults to in. SERVICE is an alias, PORT/tcp, PORT/udp,
PORT/both, a range such as 8000-8010/tcp, or all. All covers every
IP protocol for ordinary rules and new TCP SYNs for limit.
.TP
.B --from CIDR, --to CIDR, --interface NAME, --family 4|6|any
Numeric source/destination filters, exact interface and IP family.
Hostnames, contradictory families and duplicate options are rejected.
.TP
.B --comment TEXT
Printable ASCII comment, at most 159 bytes.
.TP
.B --rate N/second|minute|hour, --burst N
TCP token-bucket rate/capacity. Defaults: 6/minute, burst 5.
Incoming uses source addresses; outgoing uses destination addresses.
All matching gates precede ordinary allows. A full tracking set drops.
This counts SYN packets, including possible retransmissions, not passwords.
.TP
.B rules [--json]
Show stable numbers and evaluation order. Deletion does not renumber.
.TP
.B delete NUMBER
Delete a stable rule ID.
.TP
.B move NUMBER before NUMBER
Change first-match ordinary rule order.
.TP
.B default in|out allow|deny
Set an explicit CM default. Deny policies retain essential networking
and ordinary established/related handling. Changing outgoing defaults
exits special isolation handling.
.SH LIFECYCLE
.TP
.B init
Create passive saved settings without activating CM.
.TP
.B start, stop
Activate/remove only CM enforcement for this boot. Save rules and bans.
Start refuses uncommitted desired-file drift; reload approves it.
.TP
.B enable, disable
Enable/disable systemd boot startup without changing current filtering.
.TP
.B --now
Also start/stop when used with enable/disable.
.TP
.B status, doctor
Report activation, boot/service state, numbered rules, bans and drift.
.TP
.B reload
Validate and atomically rebuild only CM objects if active; commit
desired settings while stopped without starting CM.
.TP
.B export, check
Export desired native source, or validate it against the current kernel.
Offline check validates the schema only.
.SH SAFETY AND RECOVERY
Live policy changes normally require confirm within 120 seconds.
A transient systemd timer restores the previous policy if unconfirmed.
A fresh passive start requires no confirmation. Verify management
access using a new connection before confirming.
.TP
.B confirm, rollback
Accept a pending live change or restore its previous configuration/state.
.TP
.B recover [checkpoint]
Recover interrupted intent, or explicitly restore committed configuration/state.
.TP
.B config show|validate|restore
Show/check desired schema, or restore desired settings from the checkpoint.
Boot loads committed policy and resolves unconfirmed changes.
.SH PROFILES AND ALIASES
.TP
.B profiles, profile show NAME, use NAME
Profiles: passive, guard-only, web-server, ssh-only, isolation.
Use --ssh-port PORT/tcp for the actual management port and --keep-rules
to retain ordinary rules. Guard-only preserves policy/rules.
.PP
Web-server permits management SSH/HTTP/HTTPS; ssh-only permits management
SSH. Both deny incoming by default and allow outgoing. Isolation also
blocks ordinary outgoing initiation and pre-existing outbound flows,
retaining management replies, loopback and essential control/DHCP traffic.
Manual bans persist across profiles.
.TP
.B services, service show NAME, service set NAME PORT/tcp|udp|both, service delete NAME
Show/override aliases. Existing rules retain resolved numeric ports.
Built-ins include ssh, http, https, quic, dns, ntp, smtp, submission,
imap, imaps, wireguard, postgresql and mysql.
.SH AUTHENTICATION PROTECTION
.TP
.B protect ssh [--failures N] [--window DURATION] [--ban DURATION|permanent] [--scope ssh|all] [--port PORT/tcp]
Count supported trusted root OpenSSH failure log events. Defaults:
6 failures, 10-minute window, 1-hour ban, management-port scope.
-a means failures and -d means ban duration.
All scope still uses SSH failures, then bans all incoming host protocols.
.TP
.B protect ssh status|disable
Show or disable automatic protection.
.TP
.B protect ssh ignore add|delete CIDR
Change ignored automatic-protection networks. Loopback is initially ignored.
.PP
The monitor uses applied policy during staging and pauses during pending
confirmation. It is not a general service-log/HTTP detector.
.TP
.B ban IP [--for DURATION|permanent] [--scope all|ssh], unban IP, bans
Manual single-address bans; default one hour/all incoming host protocols.
Permanent bans have no native timeout. Unban clears manual/automatic
entries and failure history. Manual bans are not suppressed by ignore lists.
.SH NETWORK AND SYSTEM REPORTS
.TP
.B net [addresses|interfaces|routes|dns|listeners]
Read-only current-namespace reports through native interfaces.
DNS labels upstream information versus resolver/stub endpoints.
Listeners covers listening TCP and bound UDP, without process attribution.
.TP
.B net route get ADDRESS
Kernel lookup for a numeric destination.
.TP
.B net public-ip [--family 4|6] [--url HTTPS_URL]
Explicit HTTPS lookup with TLS verification, response bounds and deadlines.
No background public-IP lookup, redirects or environment proxies.
.TP
.B info, cpu, memory, disk, inode, os, hardware, time, timezone
CPU/core samples, memory/swap, local filesystem capacity/inodes,
OS/kernel/uptime, available hardware and local/UTC/timezone/sync reports.
.SH LOGS AND UPDATES
.TP
.B logging packets off|low|medium|high
Off by default. Low logs blocked traffic; medium adds accepted new
traffic; high adds packet headers. Logging is rate-capped independently
of unconditional packet verdicts.
.TP
.B logging level debug|info|notice|warning|error|critical
Set the default logs-view severity cutoff; journal audit events remain recorded.
.TP
.B logs [--since DURATION] [--level SEVERITY] [--lines N] [--follow]
Filtered journald view; defaults one hour/200 finite records.
Journald owns rotation and retention. CM does not vacuum other services.
.TP
.B updates status|enable|disable|check|run|logs
Owned unattended-upgrades policy and upstream execution.
.TP
.B updates reboot on|off, updates schedule HH:MM|default
Automatic-reboot policy and daily apt-daily-upgrade timer drop-in.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --dry-run
Propose/validate without writes or application.
.TP
.B --stage
Write desired rule/default/profile/alias/logging edits only; reload applies them.
.TP
.B --json
Structured output for supported read commands; followed logs use JSON lines.
.TP
.B --root DIRECTORY
Offline configuration workspace; never changes host firewall/services/APT.
.TP
.B --timeout SECONDS
Confirmation deadline 1-3600 seconds, default 120. --rollback is a synonym.
.TP
.B --no-rollback
Explicitly omit the confirmation timer.
.TP
.B --interval MS
CPU sampling interval, 250-10000 milliseconds, default 1000.
.PP
Durations are integers with optional s/m/h/d suffixes. Bare integers
mean seconds. Permanent is accepted for bans only.
.SH FILES
.nf
/etc/command-center/config.json
/var/lib/command-center/state.json
/var/lib/command-center/committed.json
/var/lib/command-center/applied.json
/var/lib/command-center/transaction.json
/var/lib/command-center/pending.json
/run/command-center/active.json
/run/command-center/lock
/run/command-center/guard.lock
/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/90command-center
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success; 1 operational/validation error; 2 invalid global usage;
3 status/doctor health failure or drift. Error text goes to stderr.
.SH DOCUMENTATION
Complete command and operations guides are installed below
/usr/share/doc/command-center. Source:
https://github.com/The-Specter-X/command-center
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nft (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journald.conf (5),
.BR unattended-upgrade (8)
